\begin{document}

\title{Definitional Equalities for Substitutions using Rewrite Rules}

\author{Marius Weidner}
\email{weidner@cs.uni-freiburg.de}
\orcid{0009-0008-1152-165X}
\affiliation{%
  \institution{University of Freiburg}
  \city{Freiburg}
  \country{Germany}}

\keywords{Explicit Substitutions, Term Rewrite Systems, Agda}

\ccsdesc[300]{Theory of computation~Equational logic and rewriting}

\input{systemf}
\input{examples}

\begin{abstract}

  Choosing a representation of variable binding is a central problem in
  mechanizing programming language metatheory. De~Bruijn indices provide a
  canonical representation, but developments built on them require numerous
  lemmas about how substitutions compose and how they pass under binders, and
  every one of those lemmas has to be applied by hand or by a tactic.

  The $σ$-calculus makes parallel substitutions first-class and normalizes them
  by rewriting. Modeling substitutions as functions turns its rules into
  theorems, which is what libraries such as \textsc{Autosubst} exploit, but the
  resulting equalities stay propositional.

  We register the $σ$-calculus as rewrite rules in Agda, so that substitutions
  are normalized during conversion rather than by a tactic. Two requirements pull
  against each other. The rules are provable only if the operations compute, and
  eligible for rewriting only if the involved definitions are stuck.

  We identify which $σ$-calculus version is eligible for rewriting, and derive a
  rewrite system where renamings stay first-class, because proof assistants need
  them to define substitution by structural recursion. We supply a code generator
  that emits the whole development from a syntax description. Every rule is
  registered together with a proof of the equality it installs, so consistency is
  preserved. To test the rules at scale we solve the POPLmark and POPLmark
  Reloaded challenges.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Handling variable binding remains a central challenge in mechanizing
programming language metatheory. Compared to pen-and-paper proofs, binders in
proof assistants are a notorious source of uninteresting technicalities and
boilerplate. Many representations have been proposed to contain them, among
them locally nameless binders~\cite{Chargueraud2012}, nominal
syntax~\cite{10.1007/11532231_4, Nominal2-AFP}, higher-order abstract
syntax~\cite{PfenningElliott1988}, and de~Bruijn indices~\cite{deBruijn1972}.
De~Bruijn syntax makes $α$-equivalent terms syntactically identical, and the
syntax itself is easy to define. A development built on de~Bruijn indices still
pays for a long list of lemmas about substitution composition and about the
interaction of substitution with binders.

%\emph{The $σ$-calculus and \textsc{Autosubst}.} 
On paper, substitution usually replaces a single variable. In a proof assistant
one instead works with parallel substitutions, which map variables to terms and
replace all of them simultaneously. The $σ$-calculus~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712}
makes such maps first-class objects with primitive syntactic operations for
identity, weakening, extension and composition, together with a rewrite system
that normalizes expressions built from them. The de~Bruijn
algebra~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163} is the canonical semantic model for
those operations, a two-sorted algebra over de~Bruijn terms and substitutions,
with substitutions modeled as total functions from variables to terms. In that
model the rules are theorems, which is what makes them usable for equational
reasoning in proof assistants. \textsc{Autosubst}~\cite{schafer2015autosubst}
and its successor \textsc{Autosubst 2}~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101} generate
the primitive operations, the proofs of the laws, and a tactic that normalizes
substitutions inside a goal.
% \textsc{Autosubst 2} additionally supports
% intrinsically scoped syntax, packages substitutions over several sorts into
% vectors, and promotes renamings, the substitutions that map variables to
% variables, to first-class objects of the equational theory.

%\emph{Rewrite rules in proof assistants.}
The equalities obtained by \textsc{Autosubst} are propositional. Normalization
becomes a step the user takes, so every goal that mentions a substitution has
to be brought into normal form before it can be closed. In Rocq that step is
close to automatic, because \textsc{Autosubst} supplies a tactic that performs
the whole normalization procedure, but what the tactic produces is still a
propositional equality. Recent support for user-defined rewrite rules in
Agda~\cite{cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2, 10.1145/3434341} and
Rocq~\cite{leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26} removes the step. A proof of a
propositional equality can be registered as a rule, and its left-hand side then
reduces to its right-hand side, so the equality holds definitionally. The
difference can be large for intrinsically typed syntax, when substitutions
appears in a type index and every use of a propositional law becomes a
transport~\cite{stronglytyped}. Consistency of rewrite rules is retained as
long as the registered rules are supported by proofs and the resulting system
is confluent and terminating~\cite{10.1145/3434341}. Agda applies a rewrite
rule by matching a stuck term against the left-hand side of the registered
proof~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_5}. A term is stuck when it is headed by
a defined function, a postulate or a constructor, and not reducing.

%\emph{Modeling the $σ$-calculus operations.}
Whether the left-hand side of a substitution law from the $σ$-calculus is
eligible for rewriting is decided by how the operations in it are defined. The
same choice decides whether that law is provable. Modeled as functions from
variables to terms or as vectors of terms, the $σ$-calculus operations compute,
and every law is provable. That same computation affects the left-hand sides of
rewrite rules, which leaves them not stuck and therefore not registrable. If we
decide to model the operations as constructors, every left-hand side is stuck
by construction, but we cannot derive proofs for the laws, because a
constructor has nothing to compute. Wadler takes a minimal version of the
second route, making only weakening a constructor in the syntax, thereby
successfully avoiding renamings~\cite{Wadler_2024}. But weakening then does not
push inwards into terms, and the definitional equalities that would push it
there are unrecoverable.

We choose a definition using functions whose laws are provable and block their
reduction artificially, when needed. Agda's opaque
definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling} give the required control over the
reduction behavior of definitions. A function declared opaque does not reduce
outside its block, and an unfolding instruction lets a later block see through
it again. Hence we can see the operations as ordinary functions where the
proofs need them and as stuck definitions where the rules need to match them.

Opacity settles how the operations are defined, and leaves open which
$σ$-calculus variant can be registered as rewrite rules. To make the matter
more complicated, we also want renamings to be first class, because a proof
assistant needs them to define substitution by structural recursion.
\subsection*{Contributions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{A $\sigma$-calculus.}
        We give an $σ$-calculus variant with first-class renamings, that we can make definitional in a
        multi-sorted, intrinsically scoped setting using Agda. Every rule is proved before it is
        registered, so logical consistency is preserved.
  \item \textbf{A metatheoretic study of the rewrite system.}
        Agda's built-in checker can validate local confluence,
        and using an external checker we can prove termination and confluence of the first-order export.
  \item \textbf{A code generator.}
        We supply a script that emits the entire infrastructure from a higher-order abstract
        syntax description.
  \item \textbf{An evaluation of the approach.}
        We solve the POPLmark Challenge, Parts 1A, 1B, 2A, 2B and~3, and POPLmark Reloaded,
        on definitions and the rewrite system generated by the script. Only one
        fact remains propositional: a certain substitutions is a renaming in disguise.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Structure}

In \cref{sec:pre} we explain the scoped syntax setting we work in and define
renaming and substitution over it. In \cref{sec:mis} we state the conditions
Agda puts on a rewrite rule, identify the $σ$-calculus variant that meets them,
derive the rules that follow with first class renamings, and establish
confluence and termination. In \cref{sec:fwk} we exercise the system on the
metatheory of System~F, explain how to generate the infrastructure from a
syntax description, and run it at scale on the POPLmark challenge.
\Cref{sec:rwk} discusses related work, \cref{sec:future} collects future work,
and \cref{sec:con} concludes.

\section{Syntax and Substitution}\label{sec:pre}

The development is carried out in Agda throughout. Proofs in Agda are
significantly easier when terms are definitionally equal, that is, when they
reduce to the same term. When they do not, users must resort to manual
equational reasoning with propositional equality, which is cumbersome as Agda
lacks good support for automated proof-discharge tactics. Maximizing
definitional equalities thus directly reduces manual effort.
%TODO maybe mention intrinsic stuff

This section only establishes the required definitions. We give the
multi-sorted scoped syntax of the paper (\cref{sec:pre-syn}) and define
renaming and substitution over it (\cref{sec:mis-sub}). Two features of these
definitions are important for what \cref{sec:mis} can register as rewrite
rules. Variables form a sort of their own, generated by zero and successor, as
is standard and required to represent all variables. Renamings are defined
separately, because substitution is not structurally recursive without them.

\subsection{Multi-Sorted and Scoped Syntax}\label{sec:pre-syn}
\begin{figure*}[t]
  \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
  \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \EScoped{}
  \end{minipage}
  \hfill
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.52\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \EMultiSorted{}
    \EMultiSortedTm{}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{System~F as classically scoped syntax (left) and as the multi-sorted
    scoped syntax we use (right)}\label{fig:pre-svm}
\end{figure*}

Many programming languages feature several syntactic categories. For instance,
System~F~\cite{girard1972, 10.1007/3-540-06859-7_148} has three: expressions,
types, and kinds. Each usually gets its own datatype definition.
\Cref{fig:pre-svm} (left) shows the standard intrinsically scoped
implementation, where types are indexed by the number of type variables and
expressions by the numbers of type and expression variables. Variables are
correct-by-construction de~Bruijn indices of type \AgdaDatatype{Fin}
\AgdaBound{n}, the natural numbers smaller than~\AgdaBound{n}. Thus, scoped
syntax rules out scoping errors without committing to full intrinsic typing.

The standard representation forces one substitution operation per pair of
sorts, such as type-in-type, type-in-expression and expression-in-expression.
This is the representation \textsc{Autosubst 2} chooses, and it explains why
their rewrite system needs to be extended with vector substitutions, which
bundle the substitutions over the different sorts and come with normalization
rules of their own.

To avoid this combinatorial explosion we unify the syntax along two orthogonal
dimensions, shown in \cref{fig:pre-svm} (right). Variables and terms form a
single datatype \AgdaBound{S} \AgdaDatatype{⊢[} \AgdaBound{m} \AgdaDatatype{]}
\AgdaBound{s}, indexed by a \AgdaDatatype{Mode} \AgdaBound{m}, a target
\AgdaDatatype{Sort} \AgdaBound{s} and a \AgdaDatatype{Scope} \AgdaBound{S}, a
list of sorts. Variables of type \AgdaBound{S} \AgdaDatatype{∋} \AgdaBound{s}
are represented across all sorts at once, the length of \AgdaBound{S} being the
number of variables in scope and the position of \AgdaBound{s} in \AgdaBound{S}
the de~Bruijn index. Renamings and substitutions defined over this syntax then
operate uniformly across sorts, and vector substitutions become unnecessary.

Recent work extends it to the maps over that
syntax~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution, ren-sub}, deriving renaming and
substitution from a single traversal that abstracts over what a variable is
mapped to, a variable or a term. Our syntax admits the same treatment, and some
laws of \cref{sec:mis} would then follow uniformly for renamings and
substitutions rather than twice in similar shape. We keep renaming and
substitution apart for clarity of presentation, and because several of our
rules treat the two differently.

\subsection{Renaming and Substitution}\label{sec:mis-sub}

Instantiation, the operation that applies a substitution to a term, when
defined by mutual recursion with composition is not structurally recursive. As
is standard, we follow Adams~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1} and define instantiation
with renamings first. Renamings then run through the whole development, and
proofs about them need the same reasoning we want to automate for
substitutions. We will later follow \textsc{Autosubst 2} and put both into the
equational theory and relate the two, when possible.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
  \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
  \setlength{\AgdaEmptySkip}{0.55\baselineskip}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \ERen{}
    \vspace{0.2\baselineskip}
    \ERenOps{}
  \end{minipage}
  \hfill
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.50\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \ESub{}
    \vspace{0.2\baselineskip}
    \ESubT{}
    \vspace{0.55\baselineskip}
    \begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright{}\ESubIdWk{}\end{minipage}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{The operations for renaming (left) and for substitution (right)}\label{fig:mis-ras}
\end{figure*}

We model renamings and substitutions as functions (\cref{fig:mis-ras}). A
renaming (\AgdaFunction{\_→ᴿ\_}) takes every variable to a variable of the same
sort, changing only the scope. A substitution (\AgdaFunction{\_→ˢ\_}) takes it
to a term of that sort instead. We call either of them a \emph{map} where the
distinction does not matter. A map then has a mode as well, the mode of its
images, \AgdaInductiveConstructor{V} for a renaming and
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{T} for a substitution. The operations over maps are
functions as well, and all of them are placed in an \AgdaKeyword{opaque} block.
They stay stuck, and we unfold them only in the proofs that need their
computational behavior.

Modeling maps as functions is a choice. A vector of images, one per variable in
the domain scope, would do as well, and would need the same opacity. Extension
then is a constructor and stuck by itself, but every other operation is still a
definition that unfolds. We take functions because we find them easier to think
about, at the price of postulating function extensionality. We return to the
vector model in \cref{sec:vec}.

The identity (\AgdaFunction{idᴿ}) takes every variable to itself, and takes its
sort argument explicitly because Agda frequently fails to infer it. Weakening
(\AgdaFunction{wkᴿ}) takes a variable to its successor. Extension
(\AgdaFunction{\_∙ᴿ\_}) adds an image for a newly bound variable, and lifting
(\AgdaFunction{\_↑ᴿ\_}) fixes that variable and weakens the images of the rest,
which is what lets a renaming pass under a binder.

Instantiation (\AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ᴿ}) traverses a term and lifts whenever it
enters a binder. It is defined on variables as well, so that a lookup is
written \AgdaBound{x} \AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{ξ} \AgdaFunction{]ᴿ} rather
than as the application \AgdaBound{ξ} \AgdaBound{\_} \AgdaBound{x}. We only
show three of its clauses, for a variable, a lambda binding and an application,
and omits the five that are evident congruences. Composition
(\AgdaFunction{\_⨟ᴿ\_}) looks a variable up in the first renaming and renames
the result with the second, hence it's only ordinary function composition in
disguise.

The coercion operator (\AgdaFunction{⟨\_⟩}) embeds a renaming in a
substitution, wrapping each image variable in the variable constructor. The
remaining substitution operations are the same one level up, with terms in
place of variables, and the figure lists them in that order. Extension
(\AgdaFunction{\_∙ˢ\_}) reads as before. Lifting (\AgdaFunction{\_↑ˢ\_})
weakens the images of the rest with the weakening renaming, which is what keeps
the recursion structural. Instantiation (\AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ˢ}) and
composition (\AgdaFunction{\_⨟ˢ\_}) read as before as well, except that a
lookup now returns a term. The identity (\AgdaFunction{idˢ}) and weakening
(\AgdaFunction{wkˢ}) substitutions are non-opaquely defined in terms of the
coercion operator.

\section{Rewrite Rules for Renamings and Substitutions}\label{sec:mis}

What we need of a $σ$-calculus is a finite and confluent set of rules, and not
every variant provides one for the definitions we gave in \cref{sec:pre}. We
state the conditions Agda puts on a rewrite rule in \cref{sec:pre-agd},
identify a variant that meets them in \cref{sec:pre-sig}, derive the rewrite
system that follows with first class renamings in \cref{sec:mis-sig}, and
establish confluence and termination in \cref{sec:ext}. Throughout, the
multi-sorted scoped System~F is the running example, but the construction
applies to any multi-sorted scoped syntax.

\subsection{Rewrite Rules in Agda}\label{sec:pre-agd}

User-defined rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3434341, cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2}
extend Agda's normalization-by-evaluation approach for definitional equalities
by treating propositional equalities as reduction rules. A proof of
$\AgdaFunction{f}\, p_1 \ldots p_n \ \AgdaFunction{≡}\ v$ is eligible for
rewriting under three conditions.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \AgdaFunction{f} is a postulate, a defined function or a constructor.
  \item The left-hand side is stuck on \AgdaFunction{f}, that is, the application does
        not reduce.
  \item Every variable bound by the rule occurs on the left-hand side, and occurs there
        in pattern position.
\end{itemize}

The last condition serves matching. Applying a rule means matching its
left-hand side against a stuck term, and that match has to determine every
variable the rule binds. Matching is
higher-order~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_5}, that is, a variable bound by
the rule may stand for a function and occur applied on the left-hand side. In
general that problem is undecidable, and the requirement that all bound
variables need to appear in pattern position is the restriction that makes it
decidable. A bound variable appears in pattern position if it is either not
applied at all, or applied to distinct bound variables and to nothing else.
%So $ξ\,s\,x$ qualifies, where $ξ$, $s$ and $x$ are all bound by
%the rule, whereas $ξ\,s\,(\agc{suc}\,x)$ does not.

For example, renaming a term by the identity leaves it unchanged. The law is
proved by induction on the term, so it does not hold definitionally on its own:
\ERewrite{} It meets the three conditions: the left-hand side is stuck on the
defined function \AgdaFunction{\_[\_]}, because no clause matches a term using
a variable pattern, and its only bound variable \AgdaBound{t} occurs there
unapplied. Once it is registered via \ERewriteIt{}, Agda treats \AgdaBound{t}
\AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaFunction{idᴿ} \AgdaFunction{]} as definitionally equal to
\AgdaBound{t}: \ERewriteEx{}

Beyond the syntactic restriction, all rewritten equations as a whole have to be
well behaved, in two respects. Subject reduction needs confluence of all
registered rewrite rules with respect to the already existing reductions, and
consistency is preserved when all rules are instantiated with propositional
equality proofs~\cite[Sect.~3 and 6.4]{10.1145/3434341}

Confluence is decided on the critical pairs of the rule set. The terms to which
two reductions apply, need to reduce to a common term. A rule has to be
joinable against every rule already registered, and against the reduction Agda
performs on its own, hence a rule interacts with the defining clauses of every
function it mentions.

\Cref{sec:mis-sig} proves
every rule before registering it, and \cref{sec:ext} establishes confluence.

\subsection{Chooseing the Correct Variant}\label{sec:pre-sig}
%TODO(structure, later sections): leave the next subsubsection as is.  Then a
% final subsection on the registration in Agda, containing the termination part
% and the local confluence.  "Why the Definitions Must Not Unfold" is obsolete
% and so is the vector discussion, both move to the implementation section.

The $σ$-calculus exists in several variants, and they differ on properties the
registration as rewrite rules depends on. A $λσ$-calculus additionally includes
the $β$-rules of the object language, which in our setting belong to the user's
language description and lie outside the theory we register. Our rules deal
only with substitutions (and renamings later). For the purpose of this
subsection \ag{idˢ} and \ag{wkˢ} are constants of the calculus.
\Cref{sec:mis-sub} defines them through the coercion operator, and that
definition plays no part until renamings enter in \cref{sec:mis-sig} for the
purpose of termination.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=small, row sep=normal]
    & (\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ})
    \arrow[dl, "η"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{weakening}"]                                    & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} & &
    (\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ})
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_1"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "\text{wrapped in } \_ \agop{⨟ˢ} σ"]                            \\
    & ((\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \agop{⨟ˢ} σ
    \arrow[dl, "ρ_1"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{distributivity} + \text{assoc}"]                      & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ & &
    ((\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ))
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_2"] \arrow[dl, dotted, "σ := \ag{wkˢ}"]          \\
    & ((\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \arrow[dl, "ρ_2"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{weakening}"]                                  & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ} & &
    (\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero}))) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_3"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "\text{wrapped in } \_ \agop{⨟ˢ} σ"]                            \\
    & \vdots &                                                                         \\
  \end{tikzcd}
  \caption{Attempt to complete the $η$-law against inductive variables}\label{fig:eta-completion}
\end{figure*}

All variants share a set of rules that goes back to the original calculus of
Abadi et al.~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712}, where lifting is defined in terms of
the other operators: $σ \agop{↑ˢ} s = (\agc{`}\,\agc{zero}) \agop{∙ˢ} (σ
  \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}\,s)$. We group them into four categories and name them after
Stark et al.~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101}.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Reduction laws push an instantiation through term constructors. There is one
        for every constructor of the syntax, as in
        \[
          (\agc{λx}\,e)\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} = \agc{λx}\,(e\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ}
          s\,\ag{]ˢ})
        \] They are exactly the clauses of the instantiation operator of
        \cref{sec:mis-sub}.
  \item The defining clauses of the operations evaluate a substitution at a variable,
        so they fix the meaning of extension, weakening and lifting, for example \[
          \agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,t
          \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} = t
        \]
  \item Interference laws govern the interplay of composition, extension and weakening,
        which include associativity of composition and distributivity of composition
        over extension:
        \begin{align*}
          (σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_3 & = σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} (σ_2 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_3)                       \\
          (t \agop{∙ˢ} σ_1) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2   & = (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2)
        \end{align*}
  \item The monad laws relate instantiation to composition. Identity states
        $t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ} = t$, and compositionality fuses two
        instantiations into one, \[
          (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ} = t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1
          \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}
        \]
\end{itemize}

In our model, where substitutions are functions, the reduction laws and the
defining clauses of the operations hold by computation. The monad laws are
proved by induction on the term and the interference laws are proved using
function extensionality.

The original $σ$-calculus stablishes confluence on ground terms only. That does
not suffice for a rewrite system inside a proof assistant, because a rule
applies wherever the type checker finds a match and the terms it encounters
contain metavariables.

$σ_{SP}$~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163, Stark:2020:Mechanising} repairs this by
adding new rules. The $η$-law equates a substitution with its value at the first variable
extended by its restriction to the remaining ones:
$(\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ}\,s \agop{⨟ˢ} σ) = σ$.
They can be seen as surjective pairing for substitutions. With the $η$-laws the calculus is even complete for a model
in which substitutions are also functions from variables to terms. Every equation
that holds is derivable from the
rules~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163, Stark:2020:Mechanising}.

The downside is, that the $η$-laws clash with inductive variables. The
left-hand side of $η$ mentions $σ$ twice, under a lookup and under a
composition, so every rule that evaluates either of the two overlaps $η$.

Inductive variables force the law $x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,s\,\ag{]ˢ} =
  \agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,x)$. With the variables generated by $\agc{zero}$ and
$\agc{suc}$, the weakened variable and $\agc{suc}\,x$ are two terms for the
same variable, and pattern matching on variables needs the constructor form to
be the normal form.

Now instantiate $σ$ in $η$ with a weakening, which opens the pair in the top
row of \cref{fig:eta-completion}. No rule collapses the extension on its right,
because the only rules matching an extension are the $η$-laws and both need the
head to be $\agc{zero}$. Joining it means adding the rule $ρ_1$ of the figure,
and that rule has an extension on its left-hand side. Distributivity fires on
an extension that is the left operand of a composition, so $ρ_1$ and
distributivity overlap and the middle row is their pair. The rule $ρ_2$ that
joins that one carries a lookup in the head, so instantiating $σ$ with a
weakening evaluates the lookup and reaches the bottom row, which is the top row
with one more $\agc{suc}$.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:eta-inductive}
  Alongside inductive variables the $η$-laws leave non-joinable critical pairs
  that cannot be finitely completed.
\end{observation}

$σ_{SP}$ has no $\agc{suc}$. The $n$-th
variable is represented as $\agc{zero}$ under $n$ weakenings, so a lookup at a weakening is
already a normal form, and oriented as rewrite rules using \AgdaKeyword{postulate} its equations together
with the $η$-laws pass the confluence checker.

If we wish to eliminate the postulates to guarantee consistency, we need
weakening inside the term syntax, which is exactly the work of
Wadler~\cite{Wadler_2024}. But then we have the issue that weakening does not
push into terms. Hence we would never be able to instantiate all laws we
require with proofs, to preserve consistency.

\textsc{Autosubst} can combine inductive variables with
the $η$-laws because a tactic applies its equations where the
user directs them using tactics. Confluence on metavariables is not a critical property for tactics.

$σ_\Uparrow$ treats lifting as a primitive operation and is confluent on open
terms~\cite{10.1145/226643.226675}. Its variables are generated inductively,
and a weakened variable reduces to its successor, which is what we require.
The downside is
that the $η$-laws hold only propositionally, and thus the calculus is not comeplete. We forfeit completeness in any
case because we need renamings as a second kind of map, and the transfer
between renamings and substitutions is incomplete. Hardin et al.~\cite{HMP98}
tabulate the 23 rules of $σ_\Uparrow$, which fall into the four categories above
together with a family of rules under a composition. Composition is
associative, and associativity normalizes it to the right, so a rule that
matches a composition, such as the interference law $\mathsf{interact}$,
$\ag{wkˢ}\,s \agop{⨟ˢ} (t \agop{∙ˢ} σ) = σ$, does not fire when its redex
sits inside a right-nested composition, $\ag{wkˢ}\,s \agop{⨟ˢ} ((t
  \agop{∙ˢ} σ) \agop{⨟ˢ} τ)$. Completion modulo an associative operator repairs
this by restating each such rule under a
composition~\cite{PetersonStickel1981}, here $\ag{wkˢ}\,s \agop{⨟ˢ} ((t
  \agop{∙ˢ} σ) \agop{⨟ˢ} τ) = σ \agop{⨟ˢ} τ$. One level suffices, because
associativity turns any deeper nesting $((t \agop{∙ˢ} σ) \agop{⨟ˢ} τ)
  \agop{⨟ˢ} τ′$ back into $(t \agop{∙ˢ} σ) \agop{⨟ˢ} (τ \agop{⨟ˢ} τ′)$, which
the restated rule matches.

None of these variants provides renamings, which are required for termination
in proof assistants. The standard repair is to define renamings
first~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1}. \textsc{Autosubst~2} takes the further step of
making renamings first-class and extending the equational theory with rules
that relate renamings and substitutions~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101}. Their
extended system is no longer complete, and its confluence was not formally
verified~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising}.

%TODO: NEW SECTION AND outdated and not rquired anymore, as stated, inline into prose or suggest alternative will to later 
\subsection{Extension with Renamings}\label{sec:mis-sig}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=tiny, row sep=normal]
    & (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[dl, "\text{weakening}"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{fold}"]                 & \\
    \agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}) & &
    x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } κ_1"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "ξ := ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2"]                              \\
    & x\,\ag{[}\,(ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2) \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[dl, "κ_1"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{assoc}"]                             & \\
    \agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}) & &
    x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} (ξ_2 \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s)\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } κ_2"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "ξ_2 := ξ_2 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_3"]                            \\
    & \vdots &                                                                 \\
  \end{tikzcd}
  \caption{Attempt to complete the fold for renamings against weakening}\label{fig:push-forced}
\end{figure*}

We make renamings first-class in $σ_\Uparrow$. The rules include two copies of
$σ_\Uparrow$, one for maps of each mode, with the reduction laws instantiated
to the constructors of the signature. The remaining rules connect the two
modes. This section only presents the connection and the rules where renamings
and substitutions need to be treated differently.

Renamings and substitutions are connected using the coercion operator
$\ag{⟨\_⟩}$. Lifting a substitution weakens the images of the rest with the
weakening renaming, which is what keeps the recursion structural, and \ag{idˢ}
and \ag{wkˢ} are the coerced renamings $\ag{⟨}\,\ag{idᴿ}\,\ag{⟩}$ and
$\ag{⟨}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{⟩}$. Due to that we need to make sure substitutions
that are coerced renamings reduce to actual renamings for the appropiate rules
to apply. For instance, the $\mathsf{coincidence}$
law~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising} is oriented that way,
\[
  t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} = t\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}
\]
A term instantiated with an embedded renaming is renamed directly. The
remaining coercion rules keep embedded renamings closed under the substitution
operations by moving $\ag{⟨\_⟩}$ outward, as in $\ag{⟨}\,ξ_1\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ}
  \ag{⟨}\,ξ_2\,\ag{⟩} = \ag{⟨}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{⟩}$, which we call
$\ag{⟨\_⟩}\text{-}\mathsf{comp}$, and $(\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{↑ˢ} s) =
  \ag{⟨}\,ξ \agop{↑ᴿ} s\,\ag{⟩}$.

Compositionality exists in the four combinations of the two modes, each at
terms and at variables, and each of the eight rules is registered in one of two
directions. We call the direction that merges two instantiations into one
instantiation under a composed map a fold, and the direction that takes a
composed map apart into two applications a push.

The eight compositionality rules point as follows:
\begin{align*}
  (t\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}   & = t\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}             & \text{fold} \\
  x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}             & = (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}   & \text{push} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}       & = t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} & = (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}       & \text{push} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}       & = t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  (x\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}       & = x\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}   & = t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}             & \text{fold} \\
  (x\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}   & = x\,\ag{[}\,σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}             & \text{fold}
\end{align*}
The direction depends only on the first map. When we have a renaming on the left, the term case
folds and the variable case pushes. When we have a substitution, then we always fold.

% The direction matters because
% a rule fires one way only. A fold makes the single instantiation the normal
% form, a push makes the staged applications the normal form, and for variables
% the choice is forced due to inductive variables. For example, weakening
% followed by a renaming fuses into a single instantiation applied to a term,
% $(t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} = t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s
%   \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}$. The lookup of a variable under the same composed
% map need to run in stages, after which the defining clause of weakening can
% continue, $\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} =
%   (\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} =
%   (\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}$.

At variables the push is forced. Because weakening needs to be related to the
successor, $(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$ reduces to
$\agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})$. Under a fold at variables it also reduces
to $x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$, and the only sound join is a
rule matching $ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s$, a composition whose constructor is the
right operand, which conflict the right associativity. Note that left
associativity would have a similar issue. \Cref{fig:push-forced} follows the
attempt to complete the fold, where associativity moves the weakening one
composition deeper at every step.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:fold-push}
  Compositionality laws for variables with renamings on the left are registered as push, instead of a fold.
\end{observation}

The push is mirroring the definition of composition. This is not a coincidence,
because we want to apply the definition for composition, exactly when we can
later apply a defining law for renamings. For compositionality with two
renamings this might be intuitive, but for a renaming composed with a
substitution this might not be obvious from the start.

\subsection{The Full Rewrite Sytem}
The rest of the rules deal with the completion of rewrite system that resulted
from our choices in the last two sections.

\Cref{tab:groups} counts the resulting system by group. Only the reduction
laws depend on the signature. We use $|c|$ to denote the amount of constructors the syntax has. Every other group is the same for any syntax.
The 4 groups above the middle line originate from the $\sigma_\Uparrow$-calculus,
and are copied once for renamings and once for substitutions. The other three groups complete the rewrite system and connect the maps of the two modes.
\begin{table}[h]
  \centering\footnotesize
  \caption{The registered rules by group, counted once per mode. Three of them
    are stated for both modes at once, which is why the development registers
    $72$ rules and the export of \cref{sec:ext} has $75$.}\label{tab:groups}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}rlp{0.55\linewidth}@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{\#}  & \textbf{group}      & \textbf{content}                                                                                    \\
    \midrule
    $2*|c|$      & reduction laws      & push an instantiation through constructors, one rule per constructor and mode                       \\
    \phantom{0}9 & defining clauses    & evaluate a map at a variable, the meaning of extension, weakening and lifting                       \\
    17           & interference laws   & the interplay of composition, extension, weakening and lifting for both renamings and substitutions \\
    10           & monad laws          & identity and compositionality, in all four mode combinations                                        \\
    \midrule
    \phantom{0}9 & under a composition & restate rules whose redex sits inside a composition                                                 \\
    \phantom{0}5 & push rules          & completion rules for the push strategy                                                              \\
    \phantom{0}9 & coercion            & rules that collapse coercions into renamings                                                        \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The $η$-laws stay theorems we cannot include in the rewrite system. We also
cannot register distributivity in the renaming copy of the $\sigma_\Uparrow$
for renamings, because it overlaps with associativity and only a fold would
join the two, thereby violating our push-at-V strategy.

The full rule set is available in the supplement. Because we have modeled maps
as functions, all rules are can be proven propositionally and then registered
as rewrite rule.

\subsection{Correctness}\label{sec:ext}
What remains is confluence. To this end, we rely on Agda's opt-in
\texttt{--local-confluence-check}, which computes the critical pairs of the
rewrite system. It checks that all critical pairs can be joined using other
rewrite rules and internal reductions.
%TODO say we cannot rely aon the global confluence check(--confluence-check) and why

Agda does not check termination for rewrite rules, but because we use only
local confluence checking, we need termination to conclude confluence using
Newman's lemma~\cite{}CITE NEEDED. That obligation has to leave Agda and we export the rewrite
system as a first-order term rewriting system, with scope indices erased, and
hand the resulting rules to an external prover. AProVE~\cite{}CITE NEEDED
certifies termination and confluence. The exported rule set and the termination certificate can be found in the
supplement.

Both Agda's local confluence check and the external termination check certify the rule set as it is seen from outside the
\AgdaKeyword{opaque} blocks, where the operations do not reduce. We therefore carry an invariant the type checker cannot enforce.
\begin{quote}\label{inv:no-unfolding}
  Once the rewrite rules are registered, its opaque operations must never be unfolded again.
\end{quote}
We unfold the defintions to prove the rules, but we prove them all before we register any rewrite.

Together the two properties, the instantiation of the rules with propositional
equalities and confluence, give us consistency and subject reduction. The
latter is required for proof assistants to work properly and the former is
required for the proofs to be meaningful.

% \emph{Cost.} Definitional equalities are paid for at type-checking time. Measured
% on one core under Agda~2.8, a module that does nothing but import a generated
% core costs about 70\,s, against 1\,s for a comparable module importing only the
% standard library. The figure is flat in the size of the rule set, 69\,s at 77
% rules and 66\,s at 111. Per-module times across the development run from 74 to
% 120\,s, so that fixed import cost dominates and the proof content is cheap. The
% Part~3 animation is the exception, at about 76 minutes, and there the rules are
% doing evaluation instead of conversion.
% 
% Two controls make the verdicts meaningful. Both properties hold at two
% signature sizes, three and eight constructors, which is what licenses the
% generator claim. And the setup has a falsifier that fires, because adding the
% two $η$-laws is refuted for confluence by three independent tools, as
% \cref{sec:pre-sig} predicts. Evidence at two sizes is not a proof schematic in
% the signature, and obtaining one would mean decomposing the system along the
% lines of compositional confluence criteria~\cite{Shintani_2024}.

\section{Implementation and Evaluation}\label{sec:fwk}
%TODO: vector backend; lift star; genAI statement.
We first exercise the rule set on the metatheory of System~F
(\cref{sec:mis-ex-proof}). The rest of this section focuses on generalizing the
method. We generate the infrastructure from a syntax description
(\cref{sec:gen}) and measure the rule set on the POPLmark Challenge
(\cref{sec:eval}) by how much substitution reasoning is left.

\subsection{Metatheory for System F}\label{sec:mis-ex-proof}

We exercise the rule set on the standard metatheory of the System~F of
\cref{fig:pre-svm}, against a typing judgment \AgdaDatatype{\_⊢\_∶\_} and a
call-by-value small-step semantics \AgdaDatatype{\_↪\_}. A context
\AgdaDatatype{Ctx} \AgdaBound{S} assigns to every variable in scope
\AgdaBound{S} a type one sort up, so \AgdaBound{Γ} \AgdaFunction{⊢}
\AgdaBound{e} \AgdaFunction{∶} \AgdaBound{t} reads as usual.

\emph{Where substitution enters.} Substitution reaches this metatheory through
three rules, and two of them carry the interesting cases.
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢•} concludes at a substitution, because
instantiating a polymorphic type substitutes for the variable it binds.
\ETyTApp{}
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{β-λ} contracts a redex the same way.
\EBetaLam{}
Both write $t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{]₀}$ for the substitution that replaces the
variable just bound and leaves every other one alone. It is the single
substitution, and it is an instance of the general one.
\ESingle{}
The third rule is \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢λ}, which stores the result type
of a function under \ag{weaken}, because the body is typed in a longer context.
\ETyLam{}
Each of the three puts a substitution where a proof will later have to move it,
and each creates a demand for exactly one law.

\emph{The two statements.} A substitution is well typed, written
\AgdaBound{σ} \AgdaFunction{∶} \AgdaBound{Γ₁} \AgdaFunction{→ˢ} \AgdaBound{Γ₂},
when it sends every variable \AgdaBound{x} typed \AgdaBound{t} in
\AgdaBound{Γ₁} to a term \AgdaBound{x} \AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{σ}
\AgdaFunction{]ˢ} typed \AgdaBound{t} \AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{σ}
\AgdaFunction{]ˢ} in \AgdaBound{Γ₂}.
\EWTS{}
The type is substituted along with the term, which is what makes the notion
compose. \AgdaFunction{sub-pres} then says that applying such a substitution to
a well typed term preserves typing, and \AgdaFunction{sr} is subject reduction.
\ESubPresSig{}
\ESRSig{}
Both are the expected inductions and use two helpers. \AgdaFunction{⊢↑ˢ} lifts
a well typed substitution under a binder, and is proved from the corresponding
statement for renamings, which is what keeps the recursion structural.
\AgdaFunction{⊢[]} turns a well typed term into the well typed single
substitution that the two $β$-cases need.
\ELiftWT{}
\ESingleWT{}

\emph{The three laws, and what they cost elsewhere.} The three demands are
discharged by the three laws below, printed with their proofs.
\ELawsUsed{}
Every one is \AgdaInductiveConstructor{refl}. In a de~Bruijn development not
one of them is. Each is a lemma proved by induction on the term and then
applied by hand at every site that needs it, and \ag{subst-commute},
the classic substitution lemma, is normally the longest of the three. Here none
of them is a lemma at all. Each is a normalization instead. The rules turn both
sides into the same normal form, and \AgdaInductiveConstructor{refl} sees that
they agree. Written out for \ag{subst-commute}, the two branches
are {\small\begin{align*}
            & (t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}\,\ag{]₀}
      \;=\; (t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}                          \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,(σ \agop{↑ˢ} s) \agop{⨟ˢ} (t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ})\,\ag{]ˢ} \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{compositionalityˢˢ}} \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} (σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{idˢ})\,\ag{]ˢ}               \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{lift-cons}}          \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                    \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{comp-idᵣ}}           \\[3pt]
            & (t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{]₀})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}
      \;=\; (t\,\ag{[}\,t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                          \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,(t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                   \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{compositionalityˢˢ}} \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{idˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ)\,\ag{]ˢ}               \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{dist}}               \\
      \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                    \quad\text{\scriptsize\ag{comp-idₗ}}
    \end{align*}}%
Five rules and no user intervention. Every intermediate term above is checked
against the endpoint in the supplement, so the trace is the one Agda takes. A
normal form is reached when no traversal can be pushed further, no composition
can be fused, and no embedded renaming is left in a substitution. On a closed
term the substitution disappears entirely. On an abstract $t$ it survives as a
single application to a right-nested, identity-free map, as here.

\emph{The three clauses that spend them.} Each clause can now be read against
the rule that created its demand. In the \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢λ} case the
induction hypothesis types the body at $(\ag{weaken}\ t')\,\ag{[}\,σ
  \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ}$, because that is where \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢λ}
put it, while \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢λ} demands
$\ag{weaken}\,(t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})$ to rebuild the abstraction. That is
\ag{wk-comm}.
\ECaseLam{}
In the \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢•} case the goal is
$(t'\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{]₀})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}$ and the three sub-derivations
supply $(t'\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ}
  s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}\,\ag{]₀}$, which is
\ag{subst-commute}.
\ECaseTApp{}
In the $β$-case for \AgdaInductiveConstructor{λx\_},
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢λ} has stored the result type weakened, so
\AgdaFunction{sub-pres} delivers the contractum at $(\ag{weaken}\ t_2)\,\ag{[}\,e_2\,\ag{]₀}$ where the goal is plain $t_2$, which is
\ag{wk-cancel}.
\ECaseBeta{}
No law is named in any of the three clauses. Each is a bare constructor
application, and the equation it would otherwise carry has already been
discharged by conversion.

Every other clause of either proof needs nothing, and for two different
reasons. \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢Λ}, \AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢·} and
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{⊢*} conclude at a type the substitution never has to
be moved through, so no equation arises. The $β$-case for
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{Λα\_} does face one, $t'\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{]₀}$ against
$t'\,\ag{[}\,t \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$, but the two are the same term by
the definition of single substitution.

The three are not special. Two single substitutions can be exchanged, lifting
distributes over composition, and the same again for renamings. So do the two
mixed laws relating the modes, which a development without first-class
renamings cannot state at all. The supplement checks every one of them, and
every one is \AgdaInductiveConstructor{refl}.

One case does need help, and the cause is the rewriting itself. In the two
$β$-cases we pass $σ$ explicitly, writing \AgdaFunction{sub-pres}
\AgdaSymbol{\{}\AgdaBound{σ} \AgdaSymbol{=} \AgdaBound{e₂} \AgdaFunction{∙ˢ}
\AgdaFunction{idˢ}\AgdaSymbol{\}}. By the time Agda sees the goal, the rules
have normalized the type index, so $σ$ can no longer be recovered by unifying
against \AgdaBound{e} \AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{σ} \AgdaFunction{]ˢ}.
Definitional equality and inferability pull against each other here. The more
of the index is normalized before unification, the less of it is left to read a
metavariable off. The cost is one implicit argument, and no substitution law is
applied by hand.

\subsection{Code Generation}\label{sec:gen}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
    \begin{verbatim}
kind : Type
type : Type
expr : Type

-- the constructors for kind 
star : kind

-- the constructors for type
arr : type -> type -> type
all : kind -> (type -> type) -> type
    \end{verbatim}
  \end{minipage}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
    \begin{verbatim}
-- the constructors for expr
app  : expr -> expr -> expr
lam  : type -> (expr -> expr) -> expr
tapp : expr -> type -> expr
tlam : kind -> (type -> expr) -> expr
      \end{verbatim}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{The higher-order abstract syntax description of System~F that the
    generator consumes. Sorts are declared as \texttt{Type}, and a binder
    appears as a function argument, so \texttt{lam} binds one
    \texttt{expr} in its body. From this the generator emits the syntax
    of \cref{fig:pre-svm} and the rule set of
    \cref{sec:mis-sig}}\label{fig:rel-hoa}
\end{figure*}

Nothing in \cref{sec:mis} is specific to System~F. Of the seven groups in
\cref{tab:groups}, only the reduction laws depend on the signature, and they
depend on it in the simplest possible way, two rules per constructor.
Everything else is schematic in the constructor list, so the whole construction
can be generated. We provide a script that turns a higher-order abstract syntax
(HOAS) description into Agda code with the rules ready for rewriting.
\Cref{fig:rel-hoa} is the description of our running example, and it is the
entire input: eleven constructors across three sorts, with binders written as
function arguments. \textsc{Autosubst 2} does the same for Rocq, emitting the
scoped syntax together with the substitution lemmas that need induction over
it.

Several of \textsc{Autosubst 2}'s features come for free. Vector substitutions
are unnecessary and mutually recursive definitions work unchanged, both because
the syntax is multi-sorted. Inline Agda code, such as lists or products of
terms, can be encoded with additional sorts at the cost of standard library
support. Two features are missing. A distinction between sorts with and without
binders would take an additional index on \AgdaDatatype{Sort}. Variadic syntax
could be encoded through arguments to the sort
constructors~\cite{saffrich:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.32}.

Adding a constructor to a language is mechanical, because only the reduction
group depends on it and it contributes exactly two rules there. Binders that
bind several variables at once are not free in the same way. They are handled
by a lifting operation that lifts by a \emph{list} of sorts, and the completion
has to be redone at each lifting level, which is what the iterated $\ag{↑*}$
family costs. This is why the generated cores carry more rules than the 72 of
\cref{sec:mis-sig}: 77 for STLC, 83 with sums, 87 for F$_{<:}$, 101 with
records and 111 with pattern matching. Everything above 72 is the $\ag{↑*}$
family and the extra reduction laws of the larger signatures.

The script is a single Python file with strict type checking enabled,
comprising a parser for HOAS descriptions and ad-hoc code generation, and it
carries all boilerplate Agda code so that others can run it standalone. Beside
the five languages of \cref{sec:eval} we generate a dozen files from the HOAS
descriptions shipped with \textsc{Autosubst 2}, which is the cheapest evidence
we have that the schema is not tuned to our own examples.

\subsection*{Using Vectors instead of Functions}\label{sec:vec}

%%CHANGED (3.4): the old claim that the eta-laws are underivable under a
%%vector encoding was backwards; probe-vec.agda proves them.  Now states
%%the axiom ledger and why we keep functions.
A vector-like data type is the other candidate representation, with the map a
finite list of terms. Extension alone is a constructor. Identity, weakening,
lifting and composition stay defined by recursion over the vector, so their
clauses compete with the rules exactly as the function model's do, and opacity
is needed all the same.

Nothing is lost on the side of provability, and the supplement carries the
experiment. With maps as vectors every law of \cref{sec:mis-sig} is a theorem
under \texttt{--safe}, the four $η$-laws included. Equality of vectors is
equality of data, so that variant assumes nothing, where ours postulates
function extensionality. We keep functions as the more intuitive model. The
laws are theorems either way, so we can report that the $η$-laws are never once
applied by hand across the POPLmark development.

The designs differ on cost. Every vector operation recurses on the scope, so
laws the function model closes by \AgdaFunction{refl} become inductions the
generator has to emit. Making the map a tuple by recursion on the scope instead
buys the $η$-laws definitionally, from Agda's $η$-rule for records, and
forfeits the rules, because the operations then reduce on their own and every
rule mentioning one overlaps its defining clauses unjoinably. The trade is made
per definition. Transparency buys $η$ and forbids rules. Opacity buys rules and
forbids $η$. We take opacity, and drop $η$ for the independent reason of
\cref{sec:pre-sig}.

Vectors remain the right choice where functions are not available, notably when
formalizing type theory in type theory~\cite{10.1145/2914770.2837638}, and they
are what the uniform renaming/substitution
treatments~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution, ren-sub} of \cref{sec:rwk} use.

The original $σ$-calculus treats its operations purely syntactically,
describing lookup itself through instantiation. The de~Bruijn algebra then
supplied a semantic model in functions, which made the rules provable and
usable for automated propositional reasoning. This work does both at once. The
model proves the laws, and the syntactic reading makes them apply.

\subsection{POPLmark and POPLmark Reloaded}\label{sec:eval}
% genAI statement.
To test the construction at a scale where substitution reasoning hurts, we
solved the POPLmark Challenge, Parts 1A, 1B, 2A, 2B and~3, and POPLmark
Reloaded including its sums extension, on top of generated cores. The
development is roughly 9,100 lines of Agda, 6,400 of them excluding blanks and
comments, of which 2,800 are generated infrastructure and 3,500 hand-written
metatheory. It spans five languages, F$_{<:}$, F$_{<:}$ with records, F$_{<:}$
with pattern matching, STLC and STLC with sums, and \texttt{fun-ext} is its
only postulate.

The point of comparison is the substitution reasoning a de~Bruijn development
normally pays for. In the Rocq solutions by the \textsc{Autosubst} authors the
$σ$-laws are applied by an \texttt{asimpl} tactic, invoked explicitly wherever
substitutions must be normalized. Here there is no such step, because
normalization happens during conversion. \Cref{sec:rwk} places the coverage
among published solutions. The claim rests on three measurements, each a
property of the whole development rather than of a chosen example.

\begin{itemize}
  \item The $η$-laws, the ones we cannot register, are applied by hand zero times.
        Their absence is not felt.
  \item No proof uses a \texttt{rewrite} clause. Thirty transports remain across the
        metatheory modules, of which exactly two are along a substitution equation, and
        both are the same lemma.
  \item That lemma, which we call \texttt{[]-as-ren}, is a single law, and its failure
        is not an accident of the tooling. It says
        \[
          t\,\ag{[}\,\agc{`}\,x\,\ag{]₀} \;=\; t\,\ag{[}\,x \agop{∙ᴿ} \ag{idᴿ}\,\ag{]ᴿ}
        \]
        and the two sides are two distinct normal forms. The left one unfolds to
        $t\,\ag{[}\,(\agc{`}\,x) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$, which is
        extension-shaped, while $\mathsf{coincidence}$ needs a syntactic
        $\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}$ to collapse into mode~V. The rule that would bridge them is
        $(\agc{`}\,x) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \to \ag{⟨}\,x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{⟩}$,
        in the same $\mathsf{S}\to\mathsf{R}$ direction as the rest of the coercion
        group. Registering it costs four non-joinable pairs, and the one that survives
        every attempt to complete the system is
        \[
          (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{↑ᴿ} s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,(\agc{`}\,y) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ_1\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ}
        \]
        whose reducts meet only if $\mathsf{lift\text{-}cons}^{\mathsf{R}}$ can fire,
        which needs composition at a variable to fold. It pushes, because folding there
        overlaps $x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ} = \agc{suc}\,x$ unjoinably. The one
        hand-proved substitution fact is therefore the push-at-V decision seen from the
        substitution side, not an independent gap, and we supply the missing join as an
        induction on the term.
\end{itemize}

The system leaves two costs in place. First, a function defined by recursion
over the syntax does not commute with substitution for free. The size and shape
measures the Challenge needs still take their own induction, because with an
abstract argument the reduction laws do not fire. Second, a rewrite system
constrains what may be matched as well as what may be proved. An earlier
version of our transitivity proof for subtyping recursed on
$Q\,[\,ξ\,]^{\mathsf{R}}$ to make the induction structural in $Q$. It type
checks in the ordinary sense and is then rejected by
\texttt{--local-confluence-check}. Matching on $Q$ puts
$(\forall\!\!\ldots)\,[\,ξ\,]^{\mathsf{R}}$ into the clause's left-hand side,
where it overlaps a reduction law unjoinably.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:rwk}

Three lines of work meet here. Solutions to the POPLmark Challenge fix the
benchmark our evaluation is measured against, generic and generated syntax
libraries are what we would replace, and a small recent literature attacks the
same tension between provability and definitional behaviour from the semantic
side.

\emph{POPLmark solutions.} Fifteen solutions to the Challenge have been
published. Of those, four ever attempted the records extension, three the
pattern-matching extension, and four Part~3. Berghofer's Isabelle/HOL de~Bruijn
development is the only one covering Parts~1, 2 and~3 together, and it animates
the congruence-rule presentation because its code generator cannot execute the
challenge's evaluation contexts. We cover 1A, 1B, 2A and~2B, and answer Part~3
as a decision procedure for the evaluation-context relation, machine-checking
all seven tests of the challenge's own \texttt{step.poplmark}, at the cost of
running on 2B without the pattern \texttt{let}. On POPLmark Reloaded we join
Allais's generic-syntax Agda solution, Beluga and the two Rocq
\textsc{Autosubst} solutions as a complete STLC and STLC$+$ solution. Allais
additionally proves completeness of the inductive characterization of strong
normalization, which we do not. The nearest neighbours in technique are Schäfer
and Stark's Rocq de~Bruijn solutions, by the authors of \textsc{Autosubst} and
of the Reloaded benchmark. Coverage is what makes the claim about the
substitution reasoning underneath it testable.

We have discussed \textsc{Autosubst} and related
research~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101, schafer2015autosubst,
  Stark:2020:Mechanising}, user-defined rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3434341,
  cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2, leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26} and opaque
definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling} throughout this work.

In recent work on local rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3776704}, the authors also
rewrite $σ$-calculus rules with support for first-class renamings in Rocq. They
too model renamings and substitutions as functions, but cannot rewrite all laws
against that model, because with local rewrite rules the instantiation of the
equations must hold definitionally rather than propositionally to maintain
consistency. Rocq also has global rewrite
rules~\cite{leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26}, and we expect our approach to
translate. Rocq, however, cannot instantiate rewrite rules with proofs, so the
consistency guarantee would be lost.

Kaposi and Pujet obtain a definitional substitution calculus by a semantic
route~\cite{10.1145/3747535}, presenting the initial category with families so
that substitutions are metatheoretical functions inside strict presheaves over
the syntax of a dependent type theory. Composition of substitutions is then
composition of functions, so the laws hold by conversion and most of the
transports that an indexed-inductive presentation forces disappear. Their
strictified syntax has the induction principle of the initial model, which a
shallow embedding of the same syntax does not. In their formalization, ten of
the eleven equations of the strictified category with families hold by
reflexivity. The eleventh is surjective pairing for substitutions. We reach
that same law from the syntactic side, where inductive variables destroy its
left-hand side. Neither development makes surjective pairing definitional. They
derive lifting as $(γ \circ p, q)$ instead of taking it as primitive, so
$\ag{idˢ} \agop{↑ˢ} s = \ag{idˢ}$ fails for them, for the reason
\cref{sec:pre-sig} gives. We keep lifting primitive and keep that law. They
also obtain the law for the empty context, which we cannot register, because
the statement that any two substitutions out of the empty scope agree has no
left-hand side to match. Their strictness comes from the presentation rather
than from rewriting, but rewrite rules still carry their encoding of
observational type theory and of the quotient inductive-inductive syntax, and
those rules are postulated rather than proved. We prove every rule before
registering it, and we establish confluence and termination for the result.

Chen, Nordvall Forsberg and Tsai~\cite{10.1145/3779031.3779090} give an
intrinsic representation of type theory in Cubical Agda as quotient
inductive-inductive-recursive types, and obtain a syntax that needs no
transports, no postulates and no custom rewrite rules. Where they replace the
rewrite rules by a quotient, we keep the plain inductive type and pay for it
with a confluence obligation.

Generators for binder boilerplate predate and accompany \textsc{Autosubst}.
Needle \& Knot~\cite{10.1007/978-3-662-49498-1_17} generates the boilerplate
for complex binding forms, including the relational lemmas that
\textsc{Autosubst} leaves out, but does not aim at an equational theory of
substitution and so has no equations to turn into rules. Strongly typed term
representations in Rocq~\cite{stronglytyped} are the ancestor of the scoped
syntax we use.

Another line of research concerns generic programming
approaches~\cite{allais2021typescopesafeuniverse, 10.1145/3018610.3018613} that
build syntax from general building blocks supporting sums, products and
binders, and thereby abstract over renamings and substitutions. The approach
carries over to a scoped, multi-sorted
setting~\cite{saffrich:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.32}. We believe generic programming and
rewriting the $σ$-calculus are orthogonal. Applying our rule set to the
universe of syntaxes with binding would remove the need for external code
generation altogether.

The \textsc{Abstract Binding Trees} library for Agda shares a goal with
\textsc{Autosubst} and is used, for instance, in Siek's
work~\cite{siek2021parameterized}. It builds an object language from a generic
%%CHANGED (related work): one clause trimmed, for the page budget.
syntax, but supports only extrinsic scoping and single-sorted syntax, and
focuses on generic theorems about substitution. Its experimental support for
rewriting with $σ$-calculus laws misses rules and does not pass the confluence
check.

Wadler experiments with explicit substitutions and implements weakening as a
constructor, thereby avoiding renamings~\cite{Wadler_2024}. He rewrites some of
the $σ$-calculus laws but does not pass the confluence checker.
\Cref{sec:introduction} discussed what that representation costs.

Wadler and colleagues also investigate a uniform treatment of renamings and
substitutions in a simply typed setting~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution},
originating in work by McBride~\cite{ren-sub}. It unifies repeated similar
lemmas, merging all four compositionality lemmas into one, by hiding the
structural dependence of renamings and substitutions in a uniform,
\AgdaDatatype{Mode}-indexed vector, and lets the termination checker see that
dependence where it matters. Saffrich~\cite{saffrich:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.32}
instead hides it behind instance resolution, in a scoped setting where the
uniform structure is again a function, and adds reflection-based code
generation for the syntax-dependent lemmas, which could be adopted here in
place of our external generator.

Adapting our approach to a setting where renamings and substitutions are
treated uniformly is a logical next step, but the equational theory would have
to be extended in non-obvious ways to support uniform proofs over both. It
would also require a two-level rewrite system, one on the type level,
normalizing expressions in the lattice of \AgdaDatatype{Mode}s generated by
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{V} $\sqsubseteq$
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{T}~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution}, and one for the
adapted $σ$-calculus. In our experiments the interaction of the two layers made
confluence hard to preserve.

\section{Future Work}\label{sec:future}

\subsection*{Opaque Definitions for Rewriting}
The construction suggests a general recipe for integrating normalization for
suitable algebraic structures into a proof assistant: define operations for the
structure; establish a confluent rewrite system over them; instantiate the
structure with an underlying model and prove the laws inside
\AgdaKeyword{opaque} blocks; and, with the model hidden, \AgdaKeyword{REWRITE}
the equations.

Classical sets are one candidate. Reasoning about them in Agda usually means
lists up to permutation together with a uniqueness predicate, which is tedious
and generates a great deal of uninteresting code. Their characteristic function
supplies the model the recipe asks for. Sets become \ESetProp\, for some
$\mathcal{A}$ : \AgdaDatatype{Set} \AgdaBound{ℓ}, the operations become opaque
functions \ESetOps{} and laws such as \ESetLawEx{} become provable and
registrable. The boolean ring interpretation~\cite{HSIANG1985255} is a natural
starting point for the rule set, but it needs rewriting modulo associativity
and commutativity. Dedukti~\cite{assaf2023deduktilogicalframeworkbased}
supports this and Agda does not, which is the missing ingredient.

\subsection*{Implications for Intrinsically Typed Syntax}\label{sec:future-int}
The construction applies as it stands to intrinsically typed syntax whose type
index lives in \AgdaDatatype{Set}. Richer syntax, where a substitution appears
in the type index, is out of its reach for now, and that is where the
phenomenon known as transport hell is worst. The transfer lemma
(\AgdaFunction{subst} in Agda) then has to carry every step of every proof.
Intrinsically typed System~F has been formalized in Agda before, executably and
in full~\cite{10.1007/978-3-030-33636-3_10}, and a binary logical relation over
it~\cite{10.1145/3678000.3678201} is a close example of the cost. Type-in-type
and expression-in-expression reasoning stays easy, but type-in-expression
substitution also affects the index of the expression substituted into, and the
authors spend thousands of lines on the resulting transports. Rewriting the
$σ$-calculus rules on the syntax of the typing index should remove them, and
should generalize to other intrinsically typed formalizations.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:con}

We have turned the normalization procedure of the $σ$-calculus into
definitional equalities in Agda. The operations must compute, so that the laws
are provable, and stay inert, so that the rules match. Opaque definitions give
both.

The theory we register is $σ_\Uparrow$ instantiated once per mode, with the
rules that first-class renamings force between the two. Which laws survive is
forced, because native inductive variables and surjective pairing cannot
coexist in one rewrite system. Agda certifies local confluence and AProVE
termination, a generator emits it from a syntax description, and the POPLmark
challenges exercise it at scale.

